---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeCard from '../components/CodeCard.astro';

const initCmd = `nuthatch init 0xA0b86991c6218b36c1D19D4a2e9Eb0cE3606eB48 \\
  --alias usdc --chain mainnet`;

const sqlCurl = `curl -G 'http://127.0.0.1:8288/sql' \\
  --data-urlencode 'q=SELECT "to", value FROM usdc__transfer \\
ORDER BY block_number DESC, log_index DESC LIMIT 20'`;

// Story 2 - a published nest.
const initFrom = `nuthatch init --from https://github.com/nuthatch-org/horizon-nest`;

const indexersQuery = `select * from indexers order by cast(rewards as double) desc limit 20;`;

// Story 3 - a local agent. The MCP server and its schema/sql tools work today.
const mcpConfig = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "nuthatch": { "command": "nuthatch", "args": ["mcp"] }
  }
}`;
---
<Layout
  path="/stories"
  title="Who runs Nuthatch - Nuthatch"
  description="Three workflows through Nuthatch's three front doors: a read-only SQL endpoint for app backends, published nests for analysts, and MCP for agents. Illustrative personas, current for Nuthatch v5.6.2; GraphQL is only a partial, parked read surface."
>
  <section class="page">
    <article class="wrap narrow prose">
      <p class="section-label">Stories</p>
      <h1>Who runs Nuthatch</h1>

      <p>
        Nuthatch has three front doors: an SQL endpoint for application backends, published
        nests for people who want data without writing indexing code, and a built-in MCP
        server for agents. Same data and the same nest definition, on hardware you control.
      </p>
      <p class="illus">Illustrative workflows, not customer stories - current for Nuthatch v5.6.2.</p>

      <p class="preview-note">
        <strong>These workflows run on v5.6.2.</strong> Multi-contract nests, every declared event
        as a queryable table, nine built-in chains, user-authored <code>views/*.sql</code>,
        maintained <code>entities.toml</code> relations,
        <code>init --from</code>, read-only <code>/sql</code>, and the MCP server all ship today - as
        do factories, the compliance pack, the admin UI, and webhooks. GraphQL is not one of the front
        doors: it shipped in 3.7.0 only as a partial read surface, exact for a subgraph's event-shaped fields
        and a named refusal for the rest, it is parked, and the default binary has no GraphQL route:
        it is served only by the <code>nuthatch-graph</code> download attached to every release since
        4.11.0. The personas are illustrative. See the <a href="/roadmap">roadmap</a> for the dividing line.
      </p>

      <div class="in-production">
        <h2>Live in production: Lodestar</h2>
        <p>
          Not illustrative - this one is real. <a href="https://www.lodestar-dashboard.com" rel="noopener">Lodestar</a>,
          an analytics dashboard for The Graph Protocol on Arbitrum One, serves its live panels
          from self-hosted Nuthatch nests instead of The Graph gateway. The first two to move were the
          <strong>Delegation Activity</strong> feed (HorizonStaking delegation events), byte-for-byte
          identical to the subgraph, and the <strong>Developer Activity</strong> chart (L2GNS subgraph
          publishes), which tracks it within a documented ~0-1%. The rest followed, and since
          2026-09-06 Lodestar holds no Graph API key and has no gateway client in its code.
        </p>
        <p class="prod-box">
          The deployment: nests backfilled and serving live on one Hetzner VPS, with Caddy
          providing TLS and path routing. Resource use is monitored against a 2 GB footprint budget.
          <a href="/blog/serving-lodestar-from-one-box">The full write-up is on the blog.</a>
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="in-production">
        <h2>Design partner: GraphOps</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="https://graphops.xyz" rel="noopener">GraphOps</a>, an indexer and core developer on
          The Graph, is a design partner. Its feedback is what led to maintained entities: a view
          recomputed on every query gives the caller a name but no speed, so an entity declared in
          <code>entities.toml</code> is kept up to date as blocks arrive instead.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="in-production">
        <h2>At ETHOnline 2026: Arcaidia (retired)</h2>
        <p>
          This one is over. <a href="https://arcaidia.io" rel="noopener">Arcaidia</a> was a speed
          layer over Circle's CCTP between Ethereum Sepolia and Arc Testnet, and its builder needed
          an indexer for a hackathon. Two nests, one per chain, reproduced his subgraph's entities
          and the pending-intents query his agent polled as SQL views, served over
          <code>/sql</code>.
        </p>
        <p class="prod-box">
          It began with a rate limit. Subgraph Studio's development endpoint allows 3,000 queries a
          day, the app needed about 120,000, and Arc Testnet has no paid tier to buy. The nests were
          serving within two hours of the builder asking for a higher limit. They were testnet nests
          that Nuthatch ran for the hackathon, and they were stopped on 2026-09-29, when
          Nuthatch stopped running nests for others. The one exception since, stopgap nests for
          BNB and Polygon subgraphs the network does not serve, is on
          <a href="/the-graph">Nuthatch &amp; The Graph</a>.
          <a href="/blog/we-host-your-hackathon-nest">How it was built is on the blog</a>, kept as a
          record, and the nest itself is
          <a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/arcaidia-nest" rel="noopener">published</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- STORY 1 -->
      <h2>A dapp backend</h2>
      <p>
        Maya's three-person team needs an auditable USDC transfer activity feed in its backend.
        The read path is ordinary application data, but it is usually where a managed indexer starts
        charging by query or by entity.
      </p>
      <p>
        She points one command at the contract and runs it on a small VPS. Nothing else to provision:
        no Postgres, no queue, no second box.
      </p>
      <CodeCard code={initCmd} lang="bash" label="one init, every contract" />
      <p>
        Every declared event becomes a queryable table as the backfill streams through history. The
        explicit alias makes the table name predictable: <code>usdc__transfer</code>. Her API can
        read the raw layer directly, put a derived view in front of it for a stable model, or make
        a repeatedly-read aggregate a maintained entity.
      </p>
      <p>
        Her API route calls <code>GET /sql?q=…</code>, a read-only HTTP endpoint on localhost. It is
        not Postgres and it does not accept JSON POST bodies:
      </p>
      <CodeCard code={sqlCurl} lang="bash" label="the backend reads it over HTTP" />
      <p>
        When the chain reorgs, the affected rows are rolled back and anything derived from
        them is retracted.
      </p>
      <p>
        What she stopped doing: paying per entity for her own application's data, and trusting a
        third party's uptime for a read path she now runs herself.
      </p>

      <hr class="divider seg" />

      <!-- STORY 2 -->
      <h2>A dashboard, no indexing code</h2>
      <p>
        Petar delegates GRT and wants one thing: to see which Graph Horizon indexers actually
        earn. He does not want to write handlers, and he does not need to.
      </p>
      <p>
        He pulls a published nest - the
        <a href="https://github.com/nuthatch-org/horizon-nest" rel="noopener">horizon-nest</a>,
        with contracts, vendored ABIs, and derived views all included - and runs it against his own
        node. He wrote no indexing code; he ran someone else's definition:
      </p>
      <CodeCard code={initFrom} lang="bash" label="run a published nest" />
      <p>
        From there the data is just SQL. One query drives a Grafana panel, or a bare HTML page
        with a single <code>fetch</code>:
      </p>
      <CodeCard code={indexersQuery} lang="sql" label="via /sql" />
      <p>
        The point is that a nest is a shareable indexing <em>definition</em>, not a hosted feed:
        he ran it on his own hardware and can read every line of what produced the numbers.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        Derived views read finalized data; the raw event tables are fresh to the tip. A dashboard
        built on views trails the chain head by finality - the honest trade for numbers that never
        retract under you. An incremental entity is different: it follows its source through the
        hot tip and carries its own applied-through provenance.
      </p>

      <hr class="divider seg" />

      <!-- STORY 3 -->
      <h2>An agent, locally</h2>
      <p>
        Dana builds a DeFi assistant. She wants it to answer questions about on-chain data
        without shipping that data - or her users' questions - to anyone.
      </p>
      <p>She adds Nuthatch to the agent's MCP config. That is the whole integration:</p>
      <CodeCard code={mcpConfig} lang="json" label=".mcp.json" />
      <p>
        The agent discovers the schema itself through the <code>schema</code> tool and composes
        queries through the <code>sql</code> tool, so when her user types "which indexers grew
        delegation the most this month?" it writes the query rather than guessing the shape.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing leaves the machine: no API key, no per-query bill, and it works offline against
        the local instance.
      </p>

      <hr class="divider close-div" />
      <p class="signoff">
        The pattern across all three: nobody deployed to someone else's platform, nobody got an
        API key, and nobody's story ends with a monthly invoice. To a frontend, Nuthatch looks
        like a read-only database that fills itself from the chain and rolls back through
        reorgs. GraphQL is a partial read surface, and parked: an analytics client cannot adopt it
        unmodified, because GraphQL refuses a whole query for one field it cannot answer.
      </p>
      <p class="storylinks">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/install">Try the quickstart</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/example">See the full example</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page { padding-block: clamp(3rem, 6vw, 4.5rem) clamp(4rem, 9vw, 6.5rem); }
  .prose h1 { margin-bottom: 1.4rem; line-height: 1.12; }
  .prose h2 { margin-top: 2.6rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; font-size: 1.5rem; }
  .prose p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.72; margin-bottom: 1.3rem; }
  .prose p:first-of-type { color: var(--text); }
  .illus { font-size: 0.9rem !important; color: var(--text-faint) !important; margin-top: -0.4rem; }
  .preview-note {
    background: var(--pale-rust-bg); border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--rust); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem; font-size: 0.95rem !important; color: var(--text-muted) !important;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .preview-note strong { color: var(--text); }
  .in-production {
    background: var(--pale-rust-bg); border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent, #6aa84f); border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 0.4rem 1.2rem 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 2rem;
  }
  .in-production h2 { margin-top: 0.8rem; }
  .in-production p { color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0; }
  .in-production .prod-box { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.95rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .in-production strong { color: var(--text); }
  .prose :global(.codecard) { margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
  .note { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-faint); }
  .seg { margin-block: 2.6rem; }
  .close-div { margin-block: 2.6rem 1.8rem; }
  .signoff { color: var(--text) !important; }
  .storylinks { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.75rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
</style>
